# Documentation rules

Every statement must derive from actual repository evidence: real requirement IDs, real
routes, modules, and entities, real test output, real deployment URLs, real technical
debt. Where something does not exist yet, write "not yet completed" rather than
describing an intention as an accomplishment.

Never fabricate PASS results, performance numbers, user feedback, UAT participants, dates,
deployment success, repository links, screenshots, or references. A small transparent
evaluation beats an unsupported claim of high accuracy. Screenshots must be real.

Design artefacts must describe the system as built. If the implementation diverged from
the original design, document the deviation rather than quietly updating the diagram to
match, and never document an architecture that does not exist.

Do not paste large volumes of source code into documentation — use short excerpts only
where they explain an important decision.

Do not create empty files, placeholder sections, or extra diagrams to imitate
completeness. Do not invent technical debt to fill the register, and do not omit real debt
to look better.

Keep documents synchronised with the code as it changes. When a requirement, entity, or
endpoint changes, update the affected document in the same piece of work.
